213:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| - `proposal.md`'s `## Why` carries the Phase A evidence verbatim (root cause, the red test and its XML,
214:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   the owning requirement, the guideline section; for `feature`: the absence with its pointer and
215:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   the acceptance scenario).
216:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| - `specs/<capability>/spec.md` — the delta only, and every scenario names exactly one test case. A
217:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   scenario with no case is a planning defect: fix the plan rather than promising a test later.
218:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| - `design.md` — the root cause, the single fix, blast radius, rollback, and the alternatives the evidence
219:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   **eliminated**. A list of viable options means gate 3 was wrong: stop and re-gate.
220:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| - `tasks.md` — ordered, each with its own verification. A change that touches no compiled file replaces
221:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   its build task with the structural check of the file it edits and says so instead of running a build
222:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses|   "for completeness".
223:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
224:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ## Phase D — apply
225:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
226:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| Use the apply and verification skills from the list above. Iterate with focused suites.
227:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
228:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `./tools/loop/measure.sh` is not the gate and does not stand in for it: it reads, the gate runs. Run it again only when
229:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| the pathspec or the change has moved, and quote the block you already have when nothing has.
230:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
231:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| Scoped by what the change touched: no compiled file — no gate, the structural check stands in and you say
232:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| so; test sources only — the red case plus the affected cases; code — the gate. Do not re-run a gate
233:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| to "refresh" evidence: the re-run is what destroys it, so copy the XMLs you cite into `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/worktrees/loop/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.cNOeci/cases/0/tree/case.ruBw8I/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21`
234:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| at the moment they are produced. Never mark a task done on a cached or partial run, and never while a
235:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| failure is open. Tests red after three focused attempts is `blocked`, not "adjust the test".
236:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
237:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| **The plan's first line is the cheapest check a delta can owe, and it goes first.** A diff that writes a case, or
238:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| narrows the first-line `# files:` declaration that says what a case can reach, is refused by one case in seconds
239:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| rather than by minutes of suite, and the gate's plan already puts that step first for such a delta. Ask for the
240:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| plan (`./tools/loop/gate.sh --plan --base f453eb7446742945f175a3fbbc3c9da5fae28e91`) and run its first line before you run anything else: the suite that
241:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| would otherwise refuse the declaration arrives at the end of the gate, and the attempt that paid for it was
242:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| recorded `blocked` with nothing archived (issue #792). Do not write a second reading of the declaration — the one
243:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| case that reads it stays its only owner.
244:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
245:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| **The gate is not yours this item.** The run has deferred it to a fresh child: stop when the change is applied, and
246:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| do not run `./tools/loop/gate.sh` — not once, not "to check", not to quote a number. A separate, fresh reviewer reads your
247:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| diff *before* that gate, and the gate child applies what it finds in one pass, so the item's one gate is spent on a
248:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| change a reader has already agreed with. A gate you run here would be a second one, and a second gate is how a run
249:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| spends its budget on a change nobody has read yet.
250:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
251:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| So your session ends at the **diff**, not at a gate: copy the XMLs you cite into `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/worktrees/loop/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.cNOeci/cases/0/tree/case.ruBw8I/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21` as they are
252:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| produced, archive nothing, and print the block below with `LOOP-DIFF-READY` in place of the gate line. The gate child
253:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| is handed your answer and runs the gate; the tail that follows it writes the evidence and the archive.
254:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
255:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ## What the run reads back from your report
256:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
257:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| End your output with exactly these lines, and keep them the last lines. The run verifies each of them
258:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| against the tree itself, and it starts the tail process from them: a line you leave out is a fact that process does
259:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| not get.
260:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
261:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ```
262:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-CHANGE: <the change name, or - when nothing was planned>
263:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-ROOT-CAUSE: <one line>
264:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-RED-TEST: <case name> fails on HEAD — XML tests=<n> failures=<n> (<timestamp>)
265:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-GATE: tests=<n> failures=<n> errors=<n> (<flavour/module coverage>)
266:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-DIFF-READY: <the change name — required in place of the gate line when the gate is not yours>
267:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ```
268:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
269:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| **The gate line, or the diff marker, is what ends your session** — whichever Phase D put at the end of it. `LOOP-GATE`
270:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| is required when you ran the gate, and a `LOOP-GATE` you did not run is a false claim the tree contradicts.
271:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `LOOP-DIFF-READY` is required when the gate was deferred, and its value is the change whose diff is ready to be read:
272:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| that is what the run hands the gate child and the reviewer in place of a gate line, and printing a gate line there
273:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| instead is a claim about a gate that has not been run.
274:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
275:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| **A verdict is yours only when the item never reached the end of Phase D.** Print `LOOP-VERDICT: skipped` with its
276:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `LOOP-SKIP-VERDICT` when the item never reached Phase D — the claim did not hold, or one of the eligibility gate's
277:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| conditions failed — or `LOOP-VERDICT: blocked` when the work stopped before your session's end (tests red after three
278:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| focused attempts, a measurement the change needed and could not take). Omit `LOOP-VERDICT` when you left a gate line or
279:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `LOOP-DIFF-READY`: the item's verdict is written by the child that follows you, because it is a claim about work — the
280:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| gate, the archive — that you did not finish.
281:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
282:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ```
283:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-VERDICT: skipped|blocked
284:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-SKIP-VERDICT: <one of the gate's verdicts, when LOOP-VERDICT is skipped>
285:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-DECISION: <the question in one sentence, then the options in order, then the one you recommend and why>
286:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-FALSIFIED: <the mechanism the item blames, quoted — only with LOOP-SKIP-VERDICT: falsified>
287:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| LOOP-MEASURED: <the number you measured instead, and how you took it: case name, command, or probe>
288:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| ```
289:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
290:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `LOOP-DECISION` is required when your verdict leaves a person a choice — a skip on `needs-decision`,
291:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `needs-diagnosis` or `stale`, or a blocked item. It is what the item's escalation carries onto the tracker, and it is the
292:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| only thing a later reader will see: name what must be chosen between, what each option costs, and which one you
293:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| recommend. An escalation that carries a verdict and no question is a state nobody can answer.
294:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| 
295:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| **The one skip that is not a question.** When your verification shows the item's *premise* does not hold — the
296:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| mechanism it blames is not what the measurement shows, or the cause it names is not in the tree — answer
297:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `skipped` with the sub-verdict `falsified` and record both halves: `LOOP-FALSIFIED` names what the item blames,
298:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| `LOOP-MEASURED` names the number you measured instead **and how you took it** (the case, the command, or the
299:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| probe). A falsification means the tree is unchanged, so it carries no `LOOP-DECISION`: the run holds the
300:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| measurement, closes the item as mis-diagnosed and records it apart from a fix. A falsification with no number,
301:prompt#thePhasePromptNamesWhatItsShellRefuses| or with a number whose origin nobody can repeat, is read as an ordinary skip and escalated.
